data: {"item_id":"rs_131389","output_index":0,"sequence_number":866,"summary_index":0,"text":"That commit is same as previous rejected submission. The feedback indicated tool call error due to malformed JSON earlier, but the implementation might have been missing something causing rejection. Let's re-run tests maybe they had failing behavior.\n\nPotential issues:\n\n- The requirement: \"Creating a project trims its name. A blank or whitespace-only name leaves the list unchanged and displays a visible alert containing `Project name is required`.\" Our client after POST also may add blank? It checks trimmed before sending. Good.\n\n- \"Project names and IDs persist across page reloads and server-process restarts using the configured SQLite file.\" The DB file is data.db. Server uses DB_PATH env variable default to data.db in same dir. That persists. Good.\n\n- \"Opening a project navigates to `/projects/\u003cproject-id\u003e`, shows its name as a heading, and provides a `Projects` button to return to the list.\" Our server page uses button id backBtn text \"Projects\". Good.\n\n- UI labels: The label text is \"Project name\". It matches. The heading is \"Workboard\". Good.\n\n- Row boundaries: data-testid attribute is present.\n\nPotential missing: The requirement that the \"Create project\" button is labelled exactly \"Create project\". In index.html button id create-project-btn text \"Create project\". Good.\n\n- Alert must be visible. Our alertDiv style display block. Good.\n\nPotential failing: The health endpoint must return HTTP 200. It does.\n\nMaybe the server does not serve static files under root for /static/... because we have path /static/... but our code expects /static/app.js etc. In server static assets handling: if pathname starts with '/static/' it reads from PUBLIC_DIR + relativePath (removing leading '/'). That works.\n\nBut the index.html references /static/style.css and /static/app.js. The style.css file exists at public/static/style.css. Good.\n\nPotential problem: The server route for GET /api/projects expects only GET at /api/projects. For project page route, we have server side HTML not using static/project.html. The client navigation expects /projects/\u003cid\u003e which loads server HTML, fine.\n\nHowever, there is also public/project.html not used. Not a problem.\n\nMaybe the test expects the server to serve /projects/:id page that includes the heading with project name and Projects button but also the row list? Not needed.\n\nPotential hidden issue: The server route for GET /api/projects returns rows in order of id ascending. That's correct.\n\nMaybe the issue is that the POST response returns JSON with new project but client does not use location header; but client reloads list after creation. Works.\n\nPotential that the alert must contain exactly \"Project name is required\" (including case). Our alertDiv text exactly that. Good.\n\nMaybe the label must have id for accessibility? Not required.\n\nPotential that the root page must have heading inside \u003ch1\u003e. It does.\n\nThe failure earlier was due to tool call syntax, not functional. The submission commit is same as before; we need to re-submit maybe after fixing nothing? But maybe there is some missing piece: The test might request GET /api/projects/\u003cid\u003e endpoint for project details but server does not implement that route. In project.js client code expects fetch(`/api/projects/${id}`) but server does not have that route; our server only handles /api/projects (GET all, POST). So project page client script would error. However our server page does not use that script; it uses server-side HTML, not static/project.html. But the static/project.html is not used. But perhaps tests navigate to /projects/:id and expect the page to include the heading and a Projects button, but also maybe expect the client script to load project details via API? The spec only says shows its name as heading. Our server page already does that. So okay.\n\nBut the presence of unused static files shouldn't break.\n\nMaybe the issue is that the server must serve the root page also via static handling, not special case. Already does.\n\nMaybe the issue is that the server uses node:sqlite which is experimental and may not be allowed? But they said use node:sqlite. It's okay.\n\nLet's run some manual tests: create a project via UI? Simulate curl POST then GET list.","type":"response.reasoning_summary_text.done"}

